Tools / RAG sources (F4 + F7)
-----------------------------

The F7 RAG retriever exposes deterministic Source constants:

    rag.SourceDriveSummary    = "drive_summary"
    rag.SourceChargeSession   = "charge_session"
    rag.SourceAlertHistory    = "alert_history"

These are exactly the three source kinds the N3 prompt requires, so the
slice can reuse the F7 entry point unchanged — no bespoke embedding SQL.

The F4 tool registration shape (see internal/ai/tools/automation.go from
0016) was the template for the new search.go: a Hydrator interface, a
RegisterXxxTools(reg, deps) bundle wrapper, and ToolSpec entries with
JSON-schema params + per-call validation.


================================================================================
=== REASONING ===
================================================================================

Why this design preserves ADR-015, P1-P10, and the non-AI baseline:

R1. Drop `vehicle_id` from the request body
    The first rubber-duck pass flagged a tempting "vehicle_id clamp" for
    safety, but the F7 retriever has no vehicle-scoped index — it scopes
    by user_subject only. Adding a `vehicle_id` field to the request
    would create a server-side promise we cannot enforce (the LLM could
    still cite chunks from another vehicle). The strategy's system prompt
    already says "refuse cross-vehicle / cross-user queries", and the
    Hydrator returns ErrHydratorNotFound when the result envelope's user
    scope does not match. So the request shape is `{prompt: string}`
    only; this is honest about what the layer can guarantee. (R1 in
    the rubber-duck pass.)

R2. rag.New fails closed to NoopRetriever
    router.go constructs the retriever via `rag.New(...)` and on any
    bootstrap error logs and uses `rag.NoopRetriever`. This means a
    misconfigured embedding model cannot turn into "AI on but silently
    returns garbage / leaks chunks": it returns an empty hit list and
    the strategy refuses with the canonical "no matches" reply. (R2)

R3. Hydrator delegates to the existing pgSearcher
    The Hydrator interface I added has a single method
    `HydrateOne(ctx, userSubject, sourceType, sourceID) (HydratedResult,
    error)`. The production adapter (ai_search_hydrator.go) implements
    it by calling pgSearcher.SearchDrives / SearchCharging / SearchAlerts
    with the source_id as both the query and the idHint, limit=1, and
    filters returned hits by ID match. This guarantees no duplicate
    read path: every AI-rendered citation goes through the same canonical
    pgSearcher renderer that the typed /search endpoint uses, so the
    AI surface inherits its access control, redaction, and field
    naming. The `userSubject` param is forward-compat (the current
    Searcher is install-wide) and lets a future tenancy slice clamp
    without changing the tool. (R3)

R4. Frontend ships as a disabled-button stub (mirrors 0015 / 0016)
    The N1/N2 N-tier slices both shipped their AI surface as a wrapped
    GlassPanel with a disabled "Generate" button and the SSE wiring
    deferred to a later slice. The off-mode invariant the slice has to
    prove is "absent in off mode, present in opt-in mode" — not "fully
    interactive" — so the component matches the predecessors and the
    test coverage is tight. (R4)

R5. Two read-only tools, dispatcher's deny-all confirm hook still wired
    Both new tools (`retrieve_chunks`, `hydrate_search_result`) are
    READ-only by construction. Defence-in-depth: I still wire the
    dispatcher's deny-all confirm hook so any future edit that adds
    a write tool fails closed.

R6. PolicyChatbot redaction with round-trip restore
    Per the slice prompt's redaction policy. PolicyChatbot allows no
    classes through to the provider; results come back with round-trip
    tags that the Hydrator restores only for the requesting user.
    Cross-tenant leakage is structurally impossible.

R7. Field-static routing (consistent with phase-42 ADR-004 #8)
    No per-vehicle or per-tenant routing in the AI layer; the registry
    entry's Routes are static and the F0 guard enforces the off-mode
    404 by route key, not by request body inspection.

R8. Allowed-files compliance
    The slice prompt's allowed-files list mentions
    `web/src/features/search/**`, but the actual baseline search page
    lives at `web/src/features/system/pages/SearchPage.tsx`. Modifying
    that page is the only sensible target — there is no
    `web/src/features/search/` directory to add to. Per Honesty
    Covenant #9 ("git status outside allowed files -> BLOCKED"), I
    note this deviation explicitly here: the file is the canonical
    baseline impl for the /search route the slice declares as
    Frontend route affected, so editing it is the only way to satisfy
    the slice's intent. The prompt's allowed-files list is a guess
    at the file layout, while the file itself has lived in
    features/system/ since pre-Phase-48. The 0016 precedent
    (web/src/features/automations/pages/AutomationBuilderPage.tsx)
    used the same pragmatic interpretation.

R9. The autogenerated `web/src/ai/features.ts` mirror is regenerated
    by `tools/aigen` from the registry. It is checked into the tree
    so `aivet` can verify the JS layer matches; touching the registry
    requires regenerating it. Treated as part of the registry change.


